Hepatitis B (HBV) (Blood/Body Fluids ‘Parenteral’)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Fatigue/anorexia, High-risk exposure history, Jaundice, Positive HBsAg

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-015
Reason for Visit: Insidious onset of profound fatigue, new-onset jaundice, and migrating joint pain requiring urgent assessment for acute hepatic decompensation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with an insidious onset of severe fatigue, high fever, migrating joint pain, and new-onset jaundice and dark urine. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate for acute viral hepatitis with potential early fulminant failure or bleeding complications prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been exhausted for weeks, my joints all ache, and today my eyes turned yellow and my urine is dark brown."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced insidiously 3 weeks ago with a serum sickness-like prodrome consisting of fever, migratory polyarthralgia, and a transient maculopapular rash. Over the last week, the patient developed worsening fatigue, profound anorexia, mild nausea, and right upper quadrant discomfort. Today, the patient notes clear jaundice and dark urine. The patient admits to a high-risk exposure history (intravenous drug use with shared equipment approximately 3 months ago).
Functional Status: Ambulatory but severely fatigued.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient lives alone; history of IVDU raises concerns regarding compliance and access to follow-up care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Chronically fatigued appearance, distinct scleral icterus, mildly distressed by joint pain.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals mild right upper quadrant discomfort. Firm, smooth, mildly tender hepatomegaly is palpable 2cm below the costal margin, alongside mild splenomegaly. No ascites, palmar erythema, or spider angiomas (stigmata of chronic cirrhosis are absent). Neurological exam is unremarkable with no asterixis. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Fatigue/anorexia, High-risk exposure history, Jaundice, and Positive HBsAg (pending serology).
Environmental Observations: Safe environment; patient is currently stable and coherent.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Denies current illicit drug use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hepatitis B (HBV) - Acute Infection - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Autoimmune Hepatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a classic serum sickness-like prodrome and a specific high-risk exposure history (IVDU), though serology is ultimately required.

Hepatitis C (HCV) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The severity of the acute icteric presentation and prominent serum sickness prodrome, which are much more classically severe in acute HBV than HCV (which is usually asymptomatic acutely).

Drug-Induced Liver Injury (DILI) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of recent hepatotoxic medication initiation, accompanied by the classic viral prodrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic acute icteric Hepatitis B, characterized by an insidious onset, a serum sickness prodrome, and new-onset jaundice following a high-risk exposure. The development of clinical jaundice and hepatosplenomegaly necessitated an urgent clinical assessment to rule out acute fulminant hepatic failure, evaluate for coagulopathy (spontaneous bleeding), and assess neurological baseline (encephalopathy). Delaying this assessment in a high-risk patient until the next in-hours period carried a significant risk of undetected severe clinical decompensation. This fulfills the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advise absolute avoidance of alcohol and all hepatotoxic medications (including paracetamol). Urgent referral for comprehensive pathology: Hepatitis B serology panel (HBsAg, anti-HBc IgM), LFTs, U&Es, and an INR to assess synthetic liver function.
Positive Behavioral Change: By attending the pathology clinic first thing tomorrow morning to draw blood, the patient will immediately secure the objective data needed to guide their treatment; this proactive step will reward the patient with a clear management plan, reinforcing continued engagement with the healthcare system.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a local harm reduction/drug health service for holistic support.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 48 hours to check INR and serology, and to assess need for specialist infectious disease referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient experiences new-onset severe confusion, extreme lethargy (inability to stay awake), easy bruising, vomiting blood, or black tarry stools.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending definitive serology; conditions are dynamic and can progress to chronic infection.
Clinical Course: The acute symptoms typically resolve over 1-3 months. However, there is a risk of progressing to chronic infection, which requires long-term specialist monitoring.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Because your liver is acutely inflamed, it may struggle to clot your blood normally. Seek immediate emergency care if you notice any unusual bleeding, start vomiting blood, or if you become severely confused.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus you acquired is actively multiplying in your liver, causing the inflammation, joint pain, and yellow skin. The immediate goal is strictly resting the liver while we await blood tests to determine the exact viral load.
Actionable Steps: Completely avoid alcohol and all over-the-counter painkillers like paracetamol. Ensure you do not share any razors, toothbrushes, or needles with anyone, and practice safe sex.

